From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Prarit Bhargava <prarit@redhat.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jan 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] test-sockopt: handle kernel 6.18+ SO_PEERPIDFD behavior
 change

On Linux kernel 6.18+, SO_PEERPIDFD behavior has changed for reaped
processes. Previously, SO_PEERPIDFD would fail with EINVAL or ESRCH
when the peer process had exited. Now it succeeds and returns a valid
pidfd that refers to the dead process (Pid: -1 in fdinfo).

Update the test to handle both behaviors:
- Old kernels: sockopt_get_peerpidfd() returns SOCKOPT_E_REAPED
- New kernels: sockopt_get_peerpidfd() succeeds, but pidfd_send_signal()
  returns ESRCH indicating the process is dead

Signed-off-by: Prarit Bhargava <prarit@redhat.com>
---
 src/util/test-sockopt.c | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/util/test-sockopt.c b/src/util/test-sockopt.c
index 8a12345..9b67890 100644
--- a/src/util/test-sockopt.c
+++ b/src/util/test-sockopt.c
@@ -4,8 +4,10 @@
 
 #undef NDEBUG
 #include <c-stdaux.h>
+#include <errno.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <sys/socket.h>
+#include <sys/syscall.h>
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <sys/un.h>
 #include <sys/wait.h>
@@ -81,9 +83,28 @@ static void test_peerpidfd_client(
 
         r = sockopt_get_peerpidfd(fd, &pidfd);
         if (r != SOCKOPT_E_UNSUPPORTED) {
-                if (stale) {
-                        c_assert(r == SOCKOPT_E_REAPED);
+                if (stale && r == SOCKOPT_E_REAPED) {
+                        /*
+                         * Old kernel behavior (pre-6.18): SO_PEERPIDFD fails
+                         * with EINVAL/ESRCH for reaped processes, which
+                         * sockopt_get_peerpidfd() maps to SOCKOPT_E_REAPED.
+                         */
+                        ;
+                } else if (stale && !r) {
+                        /*
+                         * New kernel behavior (6.18+): SO_PEERPIDFD succeeds
+                         * even for reaped processes, returning a pidfd that
+                         * refers to the dead process. Verify this by checking
+                         * that pidfd_send_signal() returns ESRCH.
+                         */
+                        int sig_r;
+
+                        c_assert(pidfd >= 0);
+                        sig_r = syscall(SYS_pidfd_send_signal, pidfd, 0, NULL, 0);
+                        c_assert(sig_r < 0);
+                        c_assert(errno == ESRCH);
                 } else {
+                        c_assert(!stale);
                         c_assert(!r);
                         c_assert(pidfd >= 0);
 
-- 
2.47.1
